BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2597@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T090715Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T111500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T113000
SUMMARY:Automating Kernel Bug Fixes: Syzbot's AI-Assisted Patching Workflow
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:AI-Assisted Open Source Development MC · Aleksandr Nogikh · h
 ttps://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2597/\n\n[Syzbot][1] reports arou
 nd 1\,500 new fuzzer-detected findings in the Linux kernel each year. Even
  for straightforward bugs where the root cause is obvious\, the manual eff
 ort of drafting\, testing\, and sending patches constitutes a significant 
 effort on the kernel developers side.\n\nTo facilitate this process\, we h
 ave launched an AI-assisted fix generation [workflow][2] in syzbot built a
 round human-in-the-loop review:\n* **Two-Stage Pipeline**: Candidate patch
 es are sent to a moderation list where reviewers reply with natural-langua
 ge feedback or commands (`#syz reject`\, `#syz upstream`). The AI agent in
 terprets comments\, answers questions\, and iterates on new versions while
  preserving tags like *Reviewed-by* or *Tested-by*.\n* **Human Accountabil
 ity**: When approved via the `#syz upstream` command\, the human reviewer'
 s *Signed-off-by:* tag is attached in compliance with Linux Kernel AI Codi
 ng Assistants guidelines before forwarding the patch to public mailing lis
 ts.\n\n**Status & Discussion**: The system is in a POC stage and undergoin
 g active development. As of September 2026\, 30 of the syzbot AI-assisted 
 patches have been merged upstream\, more are under review/discussion.\n\nI
 n this session\, we will focus on:\n* **System Overview**: More detailed w
 orkflow description and how the system works under the hood.\n* **Lessons 
 Learned**: Based on the current experience\, what went well and what needs
  more attention.\n* **Community Discussion**: How to make the tool more us
 eful and increase review efficiency.\n\n  [1]: https://syzbot.org/upstream
 \n  [2]: https://github.com/google/syzkaller/blob/master/docs/syzbot_ai_pa
 tches.md
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2597/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2599@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T090715Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T120000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T121500
SUMMARY:Boro: Do We Need More AI-assisted Kernel Tooling?
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:AI-Assisted Open Source Development MC · Andrea Righi · https
 ://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2599/\n\nAI-assisted tooling is incre
 asingly becoming part of Linux kernel development workflows\, particularly
  for patch review and feedback generation. Sashiko has become the de facto
  standard for reviewing publicly posted patch series on mailing lists.\n\n
 While Sashiko can also be used beyond the mailing-list workflow\, its desi
 gn is primarily optimized around upstream review interactions. This can re
 sult in a less natural fit when it's applied to more iterative or downstre
 am-focused development workflows. In particular\, it's not specifically de
 signed for reviewing backported patch series locally or tracking upstream 
 follow-ups related to those changes (e.g.\, distro kernel maintenance\, ap
 plying security fixes\, etc.).\n\nThese gaps motivated the development of 
 Boro (https://github.com/NVIDIA/boro)\, an AI-assisted kernel development 
 tool designed for local-first\, interactive usage\, focused on improving r
 eview and testing iterations before public submission\, and supporting dow
 nstream workflows such as backporting and distribution kernel development.
 \n\nThis talk will present Boro's design and open a discussion on whether 
 some of its functionality could be integrated into Sashiko.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2599/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2600@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T090715Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T121500
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T123000
SUMMARY:Automated kernel CVE backports
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:AI-Assisted Open Source Development MC · Jarod Wilson · https
 ://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2600/\n\nThe Problem: CVEs\n- CVEs: C
 ommon Vulnerabilities and Exposures\n- February 2024\, Linux kernel projec
 t becomes the Linux kernel CVE Numbering Authority (CNA)\n- The number of 
 Linux kernel CVEs skyrockets\n- Red Hat customers expect CVE fixes/mitigat
 ions\, with some having Service Level Agreements for delivery within X num
 ber of days\, depending on severity\n- Red Hat did not get an increase in 
 kernel developers commensurate with the increase in kernel CVEs\n\nThe Sol
 ution: Automation\n- Don’t manually do things that can be done by robots
 ! (*)\n- Assignment of the work to the right team\n- Identification of the
  patch fixing the CVE\n- Backport of the fix (possibly with AI coding assi
 stance)\n- Submission of a merge request containing the fix\n- Validation 
 of the merge request\n- Testing of the fix\n\n(*) This does not necessaril
 y mean AI\, but it also does now
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2600/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lpc2026-2601@lpc.events
DTSTAMP:20261005T090715Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T123000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Prague:20261006T124500
SUMMARY:From Findings to Fixes: AI-Assisted Linux Kernel Bug Triage and Rem
 ediation
LOCATION:Club H\, Prague Congress Centre
DESCRIPTION:AI-Assisted Open Source Development MC · Yuan Tan · https://l
 pc.events/event/20/contributions/2601/\n\nKernel maintainers increasingly 
 receive bug findings from fuzzers and AI-assisted tools. While these tools
  expand bug-finding coverage\, they also create a growing triage burden. T
 he quality of both bug reports and patches can vary substantially: finding
 s may be false positives\, lack a usable reproducer\, duplicate existing i
 ssues\, or come with patches that are incomplete or incorrect.\n\nWe are d
 eveloping Vega\, a continuous Linux kernel bug discovery and triage system
  inspired by syzbot. Vega aggregates findings from multiple sources and gi
 ves maintainers a view of bugs affecting their subsystems. For each findin
 g\, it attempts to generate and execute a proof of concept\, helping filte
 r false positives and turn plausible findings into reproducible bugs. For 
 confirmed bugs\, Vega further analyzes impact and severity and can generat
 e a draft patch as a remediation reference.\n\nWe would like to share our 
 experience triaging over 1\,000 Linux kernel bugs and discuss a practical 
 question: which bugs are actually worth fixing? AI-assisted tools can surf
 ace many real but obscure corner cases\, making it increasingly important 
 to distinguish technically real bugs from bugs that warrant engineering at
 tention.
URL:https://lpc.events/event/20/contributions/2601/
END:VEVENT
